  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging will gradually shift eastward through midweek as \na low pressure system lifts northeastward from the Desert Southwest \ninto the Great Basin. Increasing southwesterly flow aloft will allow \ndisturbances to move across South Texas combined with moisture from \nthe Gulf, supporting a wetter pattern beginning Monday. \n\nWhile PWATs briefly decrease to below the 50th percentile Sunday \nnight, moisture will rapidly recover Monday into Tuesday, with \nensemble guidance suggesting values approaching 1.7 - 1.9\" or \nhigher. Rain chances will increase Monday night and continue through \nmuch of Tuesday, with locally heavy rainfall possible. Most \nlocations should receive around one-half inch, although isolated \ntotals of 1-2 inches cannot be ruled out. \n\nTemperatures will generally range in the 80s, but increase next \nweekend with the Brush Country returning to the low 90s. Overnight \nlows will range in the 60s across most locations increasing to \naround 70 degrees by the end of the forecast period. \n\nMinor coastal flooding remains a concern through at least mid-week \nas increasing swell periods and elevated tides coincide with the \nupcoming full moon. Water levels may approach 1.8-1.9 feet above MSL \nduring high tides during this time.",
